course: dictionary
lesson: 4356
title: Gull: a seabird and an old trick
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. «Anyone who has eaten chips on a seaside promenade knows the gull that swoops down and snatches one.» Swoops and snatches tell you that a gull is an animal, a large grey and white seabird.
explain: Gull can also be a verb, but it is an old-fashioned one that means to trick or fool someone. You will rarely meet it that way today. Check the clues: if the sentence is about a bird on a beach, it is the noun. If it is about fooling someone, it is the old verb.
explain: Test your meaning by putting it into the sentence. «Gulls are bold and clever, and they have learned that people carry food.» Does "a seabird with long wings" fit? Yes, it makes sense. Now try "to trick" in the same place: it does not make sense, so the noun is the right meaning.
fact: A gull is a large grey and white seabird with long wings and a loud cry.
fact: Gulls are also called seagulls, and they often live near beaches and harbours.
fact: Gulls eat fish, insects and scraps, and some also live inland, away from the sea.
fact: As an old-fashioned verb, «gull» means to trick or fool someone.
fact: Don't feed gulls, and keep your food covered, because gulls can snatch chips from your hand.
exercise: «On a park lawn a gull may stamp its feet up and down like a little dance; the drumming feels like rain to the worms, they come up, and the gull eats them.» What does the gull eat in this sentence? | *Worms | Chips | Grass | Sand
exercise: «Anyone who has eaten chips on a seaside promenade knows the gull that swoops down.» Which word tells you where the gull is? | *Seaside | Swoops | Chips | Knows
exercise: Grandma says, «Don't let that salesman gull you into buying it.» What does gull mean here? | *To trick or fool | To feed | To fly high | To sing loudly
test: «Food changes how wild animals behave: gulls that are fed learn to snatch food from people.» What have fed gulls learned? | To sing in the morning | To dig holes in the sand | *To snatch food from people | To fly away from the sea
test: «The animals that win in cities are the clever, bold kinds: crows, gulls, rats, foxes and raccoons.» Which animal in this sentence is a bird? | Rat | Fox | Raccoon | *Gull
exam: Nadia reads, «Don't be gullible, or the seller will gull you into paying twice.» What does gullible mean? | Loud and noisy | Very hungry | Full of light | *Easy to trick
